<Note
  track="ml"
  slug="feature-scaling-encoding"
  lede="Models see numbers, not meaning. A feature measured in millimetres shouts over one measured in metres, and a city name is not a number at all. Scaling puts numeric features on comparable footings; encoding turns categories into numbers a model can use. Both are done wrong constantly, and the mistakes are the kind interviewers ask about."
  sections={sections}
>
  <h2 id="why">Why this note matters</h2>
  <p>
    Most of the work in a classical ML project is not choosing a model; it is getting the data into a form the model
    can learn from. Two questions come up on every tabular dataset: are the numeric columns on scales the model can
    handle, and how do the text categories become numbers? Get the first wrong and gradient descent crawls, or a
    model that works by distances (<Term id="knn">k-nearest neighbours</Term>, week 4) ignores most of your
    features. Get the second wrong and the model believes Tokyo is twice Paris. Get
    either one wrong <em>in the wrong place</em> and you leak test information into training, which is the most common
    way a project reports a score it cannot reproduce.
  </p>
  <p>Questions that are really this note:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>“Why do we scale features? Which models need it and which do not?”</li>
    <li>“Standardization or min-max scaling? What about outliers?”</li>
    <li>“One-hot encoding versus label encoding: when is each wrong?”</li>
    <li>“What is the dummy variable trap?”</li>
    <li>“You scaled the data before splitting it. What is the problem?”</li>
    <li>“A categorical feature has 10,000 values. What do you do?”</li>
  </ul>
  <Callout kind="note" label="The scope rule">
    <p>
      Scaling numeric features, encoding categorical ones, where in the pipeline each happens, and the handful of
      special cases interviews ask about (outliers, high cardinality, unseen categories, the target). General
      feature engineering (creating new features from domain knowledge) is mentioned but not covered; it is a craft
      learned on Project 0, not from a note.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="plain">In plain words</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      Imagine judging houses by two numbers: size in square feet (around 1,500) and number of bathrooms (around 2).
      If you add the two numbers to get a “house score”, the bathrooms vanish: 1,502 versus 1,501 is all size. The
      bathrooms were not unimportant; they were just <em>written in smaller numbers</em>. Scaling is translating every
      feature into the same kind of units, roughly “how unusual is this value?”, so that each gets a fair say.
    </p>
    <p>
      Now suppose a third feature is the city: London, Paris or Tokyo. You cannot multiply “Paris” by a weight. The
      lazy fix is to number them 1, 2, 3, but then the model thinks Tokyo is three times London and that Paris is
      halfway between, which is nonsense. The honest fix is three yes/no questions: is it London? is it Paris? is it
      Tokyo? Each city gets its own switch, and the model learns a separate effect for each. That is one-hot encoding.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Everyday idea</th><th>Technical term</th><th>Formula or code</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>“How many standard deviations from typical?”</td><td><span class="term">standardization</span> (z-scoring)</td><td><Math tex="z = (x - \mu)/\sigma" />; <code>StandardScaler</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>“Where between the smallest and largest?”</td><td><span class="term">min-max scaling</span> (normalization to [0, 1])</td><td><Math tex="(x - \min)/(\max - \min)" />; <code>MinMaxScaler</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Standardization that ignores extreme values</td><td><span class="term">robust scaling</span></td><td><Math tex="(x - \text{median})/\text{IQR}" />; <code>RobustScaler</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Squash a long tail</td><td><span class="term">log transform</span></td><td><Math tex="\log(1 + x)" />; <code>np.log1p</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>One yes/no switch per category</td><td><span class="term">one-hot encoding</span></td><td><code>OneHotEncoder</code>, <code>pd.get_dummies</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Categories that have a natural order</td><td><span class="term">ordinal encoding</span></td><td><code>OrdinalEncoder</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Switches that add up to the intercept</td><td><span class="term">dummy variable trap</span></td><td>drop one column: <code>drop="first"</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Replace a category by the average target in it</td><td><span class="term">target encoding</span></td><td><code>TargetEncoder</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Learn a short vector per category</td><td><span class="term">embedding</span></td><td>a lookup table learned with the model (week 5 onwards)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Letting test data influence preprocessing</td><td><span class="term">data leakage</span></td><td>fit on train only; <code>Pipeline</code></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="why-scale">Why scale: distances, training steps, penalties</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      There are three places where a feature’s units sneak into the math. When a model measures <em>distance</em>
      between examples, the feature with the biggest numbers decides who is near whom. When gradient descent walks
      downhill, a feature with big numbers makes the bowl steep in its direction and shallow in the others, so the walk
      zigzags. And when a penalty on weight size is added, a feature with tiny numbers needs a huge weight to matter
      and gets punished for it. Scaling fixes all three with one step.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p>
    <strong>1. Distances.</strong> <Term id="knn">k-nearest neighbours</Term>, <Term id="k-means">k-means</Term> (both
    week 4) and anything using Euclidean distance add squared differences across features. Take three customers with (age, income): A = (30, 50,000), B = (31, 51,000),
    C = (60, 50,100). Unscaled, <Math tex="d(A, B) = \sqrt{1^2 + 1000^2} \approx 1000" /> and
    <Math tex="d(A, C) = \sqrt{30^2 + 100^2} \approx 104" />. The model says C, thirty years older, is ten times
    closer to A than B is. Income’s large numbers swamped age. Standardize both (say age has a spread of 10 years and
    income a spread of 10,000) and <Math tex="d(A, B) \approx 0.14" />, <Math tex="d(A, C) \approx 3.0" />: B is
    closest, as it should be. The <a href="/notes/math/norms-distance-cosine/">norms note</a> made this point about
    distance; this is where it bites.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>2. Gradient descent.</strong> With features of very different scales the loss surface is a long, narrow
    valley rather than a round bowl. The gradient points mostly across the valley, not along it, so a learning rate
    small enough not to overshoot the steep direction makes almost no progress along the flat one. Fig. 1 shows the
    zigzag. Scaling makes the bowl round, and the same learning rate works for every parameter. This is why
    <Term id="neural-network">neural networks</Term> normalize their inputs and, inside, the numbers flowing between
    their layers.
  </p>
  <Figure n={1} caption="Loss contours for two weights. Left: unscaled features give an elongated valley, and gradient descent zigzags across it. Right: scaled features give a round bowl, and the same steps go straight to the minimum.">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 740 280" role="img" aria-label="Two contour plots: elongated ellipses with a zigzag descent path, and concentric circles with a direct descent path">
      <g class="sketch">
        {[0.5, 1, 1.5, 2, 2.5].map((r) => <path class="stroke-soft" d={ellipse(ell, r * 1.15, r * 0.28)} />)}
        <path class="stroke ink-red" d={pathD(ell, zig)} stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
        {[0.5, 1, 1.5, 2, 2.5].map((r) => <path class="stroke-soft" d={ellipse(rnd, r * 1.0, r * 1.0)} />)}
        <path class="stroke ink-red" d={pathD(rnd, straight)} stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
        <circle class="stroke fill-paper" cx={sx(ell, 0)} cy={sy(ell, 0)} r="4"></circle>
        <circle class="stroke fill-paper" cx={sx(rnd, 0)} cy={sy(rnd, 0)} r="4"></circle>
      </g>
      <text x={ell.x + ell.w / 2} y="272" text-anchor="middle">unscaled: a narrow valley, zigzag</text>
      <text x={rnd.x + rnd.w / 2} y="272" text-anchor="middle">scaled: a round bowl, straight in</text>
      <text class="small" x={ell.x + ell.w} y={ell.y + ell.h - 4} text-anchor="end">w₁ (feature in 1000s) →</text>
      <text class="small" x={ell.x} y={ell.y + 12}>↑ w₂ (feature in units)</text>
      <text class="small" x={rnd.x + rnd.w} y={rnd.y + rnd.h - 4} text-anchor="end">w₁ →</text>
      <text class="small" x={rnd.x} y={rnd.y + 12}>↑ w₂</text>
    </svg>
  </Figure>
  <p>
    <strong>3. Penalties and interpretation.</strong> In week 3 you will add a penalty to the loss for big weights
    (<Term id="regularization">regularization</Term>), using the L1 or L2 norm from the norms note. Such a penalty
    charges for the size of each weight, equally. A feature measured in millimetres needs a weight a thousand times smaller than the same feature
    in metres to have the same effect, so the penalty hits them differently for no reason. Scaling makes the penalty
    fair. The same scaling makes coefficients comparable, as the <a href="/notes/ml/linear-regression/#interpret">linear
    regression note</a> showed.
  </p>
  <p>And the models that do <em>not</em> care, which interviewers love to ask about:</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Model family</th><th>Needs scaling?</th><th>Why</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><Term id="knn">kNN</Term>, <Term id="k-means">k-means</Term>, SVM, <Term id="pca">PCA</Term> (all week 4)</td><td><strong>Yes</strong>, essential</td><td>Built on distances or spreads; the biggest-numbered feature dominates.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Linear and logistic regression with gradient descent</td><td><strong>Yes</strong></td><td>Convergence speed; also needed for fair penalties and comparable coefficients.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Linear regression by the normal equation, no penalty</td><td>Not for the fit</td><td>The exact solution is the same in any units; only the coefficients’ numbers change.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><Term id="neural-network">Neural networks</Term> (week 5)</td><td><strong>Yes</strong></td><td>Gradient descent, plus their starting weights assume inputs of roughly unit scale.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Decision trees, random forests, gradient boosting (week 4)</td><td><strong>No</strong></td><td>A split asks “is <Math tex="x > t" />?”. Any monotone rescaling of <Math tex="x" /> gives the same splits in the same order.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Naive Bayes (week 4)</td><td>No</td><td>Each feature is modelled separately with its own distribution.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <Source
    kind="read"
    href="https://scikit-learn.org/stable/auto_examples/preprocessing/plot_scaling_importance.html"
    title="scikit-learn example: Importance of feature scaling"
    meta="Fits the same model with and without scaling on the wine dataset and shows the accuracy and the PCA components change. Ten minutes; run it."
  />
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “Feature scaling matters for three reasons: distance-based models weight features by their numeric range,
      gradient descent converges far faster on a well-conditioned loss surface, and weight penalties
      (regularization) act in a unit-dependent way. Tree-based models are invariant to monotone rescaling and do not
      need it.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="methods">The scaling methods</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      There are three everyday ways to say how big a value is without its units. “Two standard deviations above
      average” is standardization. “Three quarters of the way from the smallest to the largest” is min-max.
      “Half an interquartile range above the median” is robust scaling, which is standardization for data with a
      few wild values. A fourth, taking the logarithm, is for columns like income where a handful of huge values
      stretch the scale and squash everyone else into a corner.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Figure n={2} caption="One column (ages 20, 30, 40, 50, 60) under three scalers. The spacing of the points never changes; only the numbers written under them do. Standardization centres on 0 with spread 1; min-max pins the ends to 0 and 1.">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 720 270" role="img" aria-label="Three number lines showing the same five ages as raw values, standardized values and min-max values">
      <g class="sketch">
        {strips.map((s) => (
          <>
            <path class="stroke-soft" d={`M${s.panel.x} ${s.panel.y} H${s.panel.x + s.panel.w}`} />
            {s.vals.map((v) => <circle class="stroke fill-gold" cx={sx(s.panel, v)} cy={s.panel.y} r="7" />)}
          </>
        ))}
      </g>
      {strips.map((s) => (
        <>
          <text class="small" x={s.panel.x - 12} y={s.panel.y + 5} text-anchor="end">{s.label}</text>
          {s.vals.map((v, i) => <text class="small" x={sx(s.panel, v)} y={s.panel.y - 14} text-anchor="middle">{typeof v === 'number' && !Number.isInteger(v) ? v.toFixed(2) : v}</text>)}
          {s.ticks.map((t) => <text class="small" x={sx(s.panel, t)} y={s.panel.y + 26} text-anchor="middle" style="opacity:0.7">{t}</text>)}
        </>
      ))}
    </svg>
  </Figure>
  <p>Use the column of ages <Math tex="(20, 30, 40, 50, 60)" />, mean 40, standard deviation 14.1, as the example throughout.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>Standardization</strong> (z-scoring). Subtract the mean, divide by the standard deviation:
  </p>
  <Math block tex="z = \frac{x - \mu}{\sigma} \qquad 20 \mapsto \frac{20 - 40}{14.1} = -1.41,\quad 40 \mapsto 0,\quad 60 \mapsto 1.41" />
  <p>
    The result has mean 0 and standard deviation 1, whatever the original units. Values are not bounded: an outlier
    at 200 would become 11.3. It is the default for linear models and for the inputs of the week-4 and week-5 models
    (SVMs, PCA, neural networks), because those methods assume roughly zero-centred, unit-spread inputs and because
    it preserves outliers as outliers.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Min-max scaling.</strong> Map the smallest value to 0 and the largest to 1:
  </p>
  <Math block tex="x' = \frac{x - \min}{\max - \min} \qquad 20 \mapsto 0,\quad 30 \mapsto 0.25,\quad 60 \mapsto 1" />
  <p>
    Bounded, which some methods want (pixel intensities, inputs to a sigmoid, distance computations where you want
    every feature to have the same maximum influence). Fragile with outliers: one value of 1,000 in the income column
    sends every normal income to 0.01 or so. Also, new data can fall outside [0, 1].
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Robust scaling.</strong> Subtract the median, divide by the interquartile range (75th percentile minus
    25th):
  </p>
  <Math block tex="x' = \frac{x - \text{median}}{Q_3 - Q_1} \qquad 20 \mapsto \frac{20 - 40}{50 - 30} = -1,\quad 40 \mapsto 0,\quad 60 \mapsto 1" />
  <p>
    Median and quartiles barely move when a few values are extreme, so the bulk of the data keeps a sensible spread and
    the outliers stay far out where a model can treat them as unusual. Use it when the column has heavy tails you do
    not want to delete.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Log transform.</strong> Replace <Math tex="x" /> by <Math tex="\log(1 + x)" /> (the <Math tex="1 +" />
    handles zeros) for columns that span orders of magnitude and pile up near zero: income, prices, counts,
    populations, word frequencies. It is not a rescaling but a reshaping: it turns a long right tail into something
    closer to a bell, which linear models fit much better. The <a href="/notes/math/functions-exponents-logs/">logarithms
    note</a> explained why multiplicative quantities belong on a log scale; here is the picture.
  </p>
  <Figure n={3} caption="An income column before and after a log transform. Left: most values are small and a few are enormous, so any linear scaling crushes the bulk into a corner. Right: after log(1 + x) the distribution is roughly symmetric and every scaler behaves.">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 740 280" role="img" aria-label="Two histograms: a heavily right-skewed one and a roughly bell-shaped one after taking logarithms">
      <g class="sketch">
        <path class="stroke-soft" d={`M${barsL.x} ${sy(barsL, 0)} H${barsL.x + barsL.w}`}></path>
        <path class="stroke-soft" d={`M${barsR.x} ${sy(barsR, 0)} H${barsR.x + barsR.w}`}></path>
        {before.map((h, i) => <rect class="stroke fill-gold" x={sx(barsL, i) + 2} y={sy(barsL, h)} width={barsL.w / 12 - 4} height={sy(barsL, 0) - sy(barsL, h)} />)}
        {after.map((h, i) => <rect class="stroke fill-green" x={sx(barsR, i) + 2} y={sy(barsR, h)} width={barsR.w / 12 - 4} height={sy(barsR, 0) - sy(barsR, h)} />)}
      </g>
      <text x={barsL.x + barsL.w / 2} y="262" text-anchor="middle">income: long right tail</text>
      <text x={barsR.x + barsR.w / 2} y="262" text-anchor="middle">log(1 + income): roughly symmetric</text>
      <text class="small" x={barsL.x + barsL.w} y={sy(barsL, 0) + 18} text-anchor="end">a few millionaires →</text>
    </svg>
  </Figure>
  <p>Which one, in one table:</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Method</th><th>Result</th><th>Outliers</th><th>Reach for it when</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Standardization</td><td>mean 0, std 1, unbounded</td><td>kept as large values; they do distort <Math tex="\mu" /> and <Math tex="\sigma" /></td><td>Default for linear models and for SVM, PCA and neural nets later</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Min-max</td><td>exactly [0, 1]</td><td>crush everything else</td><td>Bounded inputs needed; no outliers; images</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Robust</td><td>median 0, IQR 1, unbounded</td><td>ignored when computing the scale</td><td>Heavy tails you want to keep</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Log</td><td>compressed tail</td><td>pulled towards the bulk</td><td>Positive, skewed, multiplicative quantities; usually followed by standardization</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <Source
    kind="read"
    href="https://scikit-learn.org/stable/auto_examples/preprocessing/plot_all_scaling.html"
    title="scikit-learn example: Compare the effect of different scalers on data with outliers"
    meta="Every scaler applied to the same two skewed features, plotted side by side. The pictures are the whole section; skim the text."
  />
  <Source
    kind="read"
    href="https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/numerical-data/normalization"
    title="Google ML Crash Course: Normalization"
    meta="Short, with the rule of thumb for choosing between z-score, linear scaling and log scaling by looking at the distribution."
  />
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “I standardize by default, use min-max when I need a bounded range and the data has no outliers, switch to
      robust scaling for heavy-tailed features, and log-transform positive skewed quantities before scaling. Trees
      get none of this.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="fit-on-train">Fit on train, apply everywhere</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      Every scaler has to learn something from the data: the mean and standard deviation, the min and max, the list
      of categories. The rule is that it may learn those things only from the training set, and must then apply the
      same frozen numbers to the validation set, the test set and tomorrow’s production data. Learning them from the
      test set too is like peeking at the exam: your score goes up and means less. And in production there is no
      “all the data” to peek at anyway; new rows arrive one at a time and must be scaled with numbers you saved.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Figure n={4} caption="The fit/transform discipline. Statistics are learned from the training split only (fit), then the identical transformation is applied to every split and to production data (transform). A Pipeline enforces this automatically.">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 740 260" role="img" aria-label="Diagram: training data flows into fit, producing a frozen scaler; the scaler transforms training, validation, test and production data">
      <g class="sketch">
        <rect class="stroke fill-gold" x="30" y="40" width="130" height="50" rx="8"></rect>
        <rect class="stroke fill-green" x="230" y="40" width="150" height="50" rx="8"></rect>
        <rect class="stroke fill-paper" x="30" y="140" width="130" height="40" rx="8"></rect>
        <rect class="stroke fill-paper" x="190" y="140" width="130" height="40" rx="8"></rect>
        <rect class="stroke fill-paper" x="350" y="140" width="130" height="40" rx="8"></rect>
        <rect class="stroke fill-paper" x="510" y="140" width="150" height="40" rx="8"></rect>
        <path class="stroke" d="M160 65 H222" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
        <path class="stroke ink-red" d="M305 90 V115 H95 V132 M305 115 H255 V132 M305 115 H415 V132 M305 115 H585 V132" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
        <path class="stroke ink-red" d="M255 118 V132" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
        <path class="stroke ink-red" d="M415 118 V132" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
        <path class="stroke ink-red" d="M585 118 V132" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
      </g>
      <text x="95" y="70" text-anchor="middle">training split</text>
      <text class="small" x="191" y="58" text-anchor="middle">fit</text>
      <text x="305" y="60" text-anchor="middle">scaler / encoder</text>
      <text class="small" x="305" y="80" text-anchor="middle">learns μ, σ, categories</text>
      <text class="small label-red" x="470" y="110">transform (frozen numbers)</text>
      <text class="small" x="95" y="165" text-anchor="middle">train</text>
      <text class="small" x="255" y="165" text-anchor="middle">validation</text>
      <text class="small" x="415" y="165" text-anchor="middle">test</text>
      <text class="small" x="585" y="165" text-anchor="middle">production, next week</text>
      <text class="small" x="370" y="225" text-anchor="middle">never: fit on all data, then split</text>
    </svg>
  </Figure>
  <p>
    In scikit-learn the two steps are literally two methods: <code>fit</code> learns the statistics, <code>transform</code>
    applies them, and <code>fit_transform</code> does both on the training set. The discipline is: <code>fit_transform</code>
    on train, <code>transform</code> only on everything else. The mistake of fitting on the full dataset before
    splitting is a form of <span class="term">data leakage</span>: information from the test rows (their mean, their
    range, their categories) has shaped the features the model trained on. The damage is usually small for a scaler
    and large for things like target encoding, but the rule is the same, and reviewers and interviewers check for it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The tool that makes the rule automatic is a <code>Pipeline</code>: chain the preprocessing steps and the model
    into one object, call <code>fit</code> on the training data once, and every step fits on train and transforms
    downstream correctly, including inside cross-validation, where the fit happens separately on each fold. A
    <code>ColumnTransformer</code> routes numeric columns to a scaler and categorical columns to an encoder. The
    <a href="#code">code section</a> has the complete pattern; use it for everything in Project 0.
  </p>
  <p>
    One more reason the rule matters: <strong>deployment</strong>. The production system must scale a new row with
    the <em>training</em> mean and standard deviation, so the scaler is part of the model and is saved with it. A
    pipeline object saved to disk carries both; a hand-written <code>(x - x.mean()) / x.std()</code> in a notebook
    does not, and the first production row is scaled by itself into NaN.
  </p>
  <Source
    kind="read"
    href="https://scikit-learn.org/stable/common_pitfalls.html"
    title="scikit-learn user guide: Common pitfalls and recommended practices"
    meta="The section “Data leakage” and its “Pre-processing” example are exactly this section with code. Fifteen minutes, and it will save you from the single most common mistake on Project 0."
  />
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “Every preprocessing step that learns statistics is fitted on the training split only and applied unchanged to
      validation, test and production data; fitting on the full dataset before splitting leaks test information. I
      put scalers and encoders in a Pipeline with the model so that cross-validation and deployment handle this
      automatically.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="encoding">Encoding categories</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      Some features are labels, not amounts: city, colour, product type, day of the week. A model only multiplies and
      adds, so each label has to become numbers. The question to ask first is whether the labels have an order.
      Small, medium, large do: give them 1, 2, 3 and the model can use “bigger means more”. London, Paris, Tokyo do
      not: give them 1, 2, 3 and you have invented an order and a spacing that are not real. For unordered labels,
      the safe encoding is one switch per label.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Figure n={5} caption="One-hot encoding a city column. Each category becomes its own 0/1 column; every row has exactly one 1. With an intercept in the model, the three columns sum to the intercept column, so one is dropped.">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 720 250" role="img" aria-label="A city column with four rows next to its one-hot encoding as three zero-one columns">
      <g class="sketch">
        {cities.map((_, r) => <rect class="stroke fill-gold" x="60" y={60 + r * 40} width="120" height="36" />)}
        {cities.map((_, r) => cols.map((_, c) => <rect class={`stroke ${cities[r] === cols[c] ? 'fill-green' : 'fill-paper'}`} x={300 + c * 90} y={60 + r * 40} width="86" height="36" />))}
        <path class="stroke" d="M190 138 H290" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
      </g>
      <text class="small" x="120" y="48" text-anchor="middle">city</text>
      {cities.map((city, r) => <text x="120" y={84 + r * 40} text-anchor="middle">{city}</text>)}
      {cols.map((c, i) => <text class="small" x={343 + i * 90} y="48" text-anchor="middle">is_{c}</text>)}
      {cities.map((_, r) => cols.map((_, c) => <text x={343 + c * 90} y={84 + r * 40} text-anchor="middle">{cities[r] === cols[c] ? 1 : 0}</text>))}
      <text class="small label-red" x="600" y="84">each row:</text>
      <text class="small label-red" x="600" y="104">exactly one 1</text>
      <text class="small" x="600" y="150">columns sum to 1</text>
      <text class="small" x="600" y="170">= the intercept,</text>
      <text class="small" x="600" y="190">so drop one for</text>
      <text class="small" x="600" y="210">linear models</text>
      <text class="small" x="240" y="128" text-anchor="middle">one-hot</text>
    </svg>
  </Figure>
  <p>
    <strong>Nominal versus ordinal.</strong> A <span class="term">nominal</span> feature has categories with no order
    (city, colour, user ID). An <span class="term">ordinal</span> feature has categories with a meaningful order but
    not necessarily equal gaps (small / medium / large; poor / fair / good / excellent). The encoding follows the type.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>One-hot encoding</strong> turns a nominal feature with <Math tex="k" /> categories into <Math tex="k" />
    binary columns, exactly one of which is 1 per row. A linear model then learns one weight per category, which is
    a separate additive effect for each: the model can learn that Tokyo adds 80 and Paris adds 20 with no assumption
    linking them. Trees handle one-hot columns too, though many tree libraries now accept categories directly and do
    better with them. Two details:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The dummy variable trap.</strong> The <Math tex="k" /> columns always sum to 1, which is the intercept’s
      constant column. For a linear model with an intercept that is perfect collinearity: <Math tex="X^\top X" /> is
      singular and the coefficients are not unique (the <a href="/notes/ml/linear-regression/#closed-form">normal
      equation</a> fails). Drop one column; the dropped category becomes the <em>reference</em>, and each remaining
      coefficient reads as “difference from the reference”. Regularized models and trees do not need the drop.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Unseen categories.</strong> Production will send a city that was not in training. The encoder must be
      told what to do: <code>handle_unknown="ignore"</code> outputs all zeros (the reference category’s effect), which
      is a reasonable default. Without it, the first unknown value crashes the service.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Ordinal encoding</strong> maps ordered categories to integers that respect the order: small = 0, medium =
    1, large = 2. Linear models then assume equal gaps between levels, which may or may not be right; trees only use
    the order and are happy. The pitfall interviewers probe is using integer codes for a <em>nominal</em> feature
    (sometimes called label encoding, after the <code>LabelEncoder</code> that is meant for targets): London = 0,
    Paris = 1, Tokyo = 2 tells a linear model that Tokyo is “two Londons” and that Paris sits exactly between. Trees
    can partly recover by splitting repeatedly, but it is still arbitrary.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Binary features</strong> (yes/no) are a single 0/1 column; one-hot would create two redundant columns.
    <strong>Cyclic features</strong> such as hour of day or month have an order that wraps around: hour 23 is next to
    hour 0. Encoding them as <Math tex="\sin(2\pi h/24)" /> and <Math tex="\cos(2\pi h/24)" /> puts them on a circle
    so that 23 and 0 are neighbours; it is a small trick that shows up in time-series interviews.
  </p>
  <Source
    kind="read"
    href="https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/categorical-data/one-hot-encoding"
    title="Google ML Crash Course: One-hot encoding"
    meta="The short, pictured version of this section, including why integer codes mislead a model. Then read the “Feature crosses” page that follows it."
  />
  <Source
    kind="read"
    href="https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.preprocessing.OneHotEncoder.html"
    title="scikit-learn documentation: OneHotEncoder"
    meta="Read the drop and handle_unknown parameters. Those two are the dummy trap and the unseen-category problem, respectively."
  />
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “Nominal categories get one-hot encoded so the model learns an independent effect per category; ordinal
      categories get integer codes that respect their order. Integer-coding nominal data imposes a false order. With
      an intercept I drop one one-hot column to avoid perfect collinearity, and I configure the encoder to handle
      unseen categories at inference.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="high-cardinality">Many categories: targets, hashes, embeddings</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      One-hot works for three cities. It does not work for 50,000 postcodes or ten million user IDs: that many
      switches makes a huge, almost-all-zero table, most switches are seen only a few times, and the model cannot
      learn anything reliable about a postcode with two examples. The three escapes are: replace each category by a
      number that summarizes its history (the average outcome for that postcode), squash the categories into a fixed
      number of buckets, or let the model learn a short vector for each category. The third is exactly how a language
      model treats words.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p>
    <strong>Target encoding.</strong> Replace each category by the mean of the target among training rows with that
    category: the postcode becomes “average price in this postcode”. One column, very informative, and very easy to
    get wrong. If you compute the mean using the row’s own target, the feature contains the answer and the model
    overfits spectacularly; rare categories with one row become perfect predictors. The fixes are to compute the
    encoding out-of-fold (each row’s value from the other folds) and to <em>smooth</em> rare categories towards the
    global mean. scikit-learn’s <code>TargetEncoder</code> does both. This is the leakage problem of the previous
    section in its most dangerous form, and “how would you encode a high-cardinality feature without leaking?” is a
    real interview question.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Hashing.</strong> Apply a hash function to the category string and use the result modulo a fixed number
    of buckets (say 1,024) as a one-hot index. No category list to store, unseen values handled automatically, fixed
    memory. The cost is collisions: two unrelated postcodes may share a bucket. Used at scale in ad systems and in
    the original scikit-learn <code>FeatureHasher</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Embeddings.</strong> Give each category a learned vector of, say, 8 or 32 numbers, stored in a lookup
    table of shape <Math tex="(\text{categories} \times \text{dimension})" />, and train the table along with the
    model. Categories that behave similarly end up with similar vectors, so the model generalizes across them; rare
    categories borrow strength from their neighbours. This is a one-hot encoding followed by a weight matrix, learned
    end to end (a one-hot row times the table picks out one row, as the
    <a href="/notes/math/matrices-and-shapes/#later">matrices note</a> observed). It needs a
    <Term id="neural-network">neural network</Term> and enough data, and it is the native way deep models handle
    categories: user IDs in recommenders, and tokens in language models.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Method</th><th>Columns</th><th>Handles unseen?</th><th>Main risk</th><th>Use when</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>One-hot</td><td><Math tex="k" /> (or <Math tex="k-1" />)</td><td>With a flag, as all zeros</td><td>Width and sparsity when <Math tex="k" /> is large</td><td><Math tex="k" /> up to a few dozen</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Target encoding</td><td>1</td><td>Falls back to the global mean</td><td>Leakage; needs out-of-fold computation and smoothing</td><td>Large <Math tex="k" />, tree models, tabular competitions</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Hashing</td><td>fixed <Math tex="B" /></td><td>Yes, by construction</td><td>Collisions; no interpretability</td><td>Huge or open-ended vocabularies, streaming</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Embedding</td><td>chosen dimension <Math tex="d" /></td><td>Needs an “unknown” row</td><td>Needs data and a neural network</td><td>Deep models; IDs, tokens</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <Source
    kind="read"
    href="https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.preprocessing.TargetEncoder.html"
    title="scikit-learn documentation: TargetEncoder"
    meta="Read the first paragraph and the note on cross fitting. It explains exactly why the naive version leaks and how the implementation avoids it."
  />
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “For high-cardinality categoricals I avoid wide one-hot matrices: target encoding computed out-of-fold with
      smoothing, feature hashing into a fixed number of buckets, or a learned embedding table in a neural network.
      The leakage risk of target encoding is the thing to call out.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="targets-missing">Scaling the target, and missing values</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      Two loose ends. First, the thing you are predicting can be reshaped too: predicting the log of a price instead
      of the price often works much better, as long as you remember to undo the log on the way out. Second, real
      columns have holes, and a hole has to be filled with something before a model can multiply it. The fill value is
      learned from the training set like any other statistic, and it is often worth adding a “this was missing” switch
      so the model can treat the absence itself as information.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p>
    <strong>The target.</strong> For regression on a positive, skewed target (prices, incomes, durations), training on
    <Math tex="\log y" /> turns multiplicative errors into additive ones: a 10% miss costs the same on a cheap house
    as on an expensive one, instead of the expensive house dominating the loss. Predict, then apply <Math tex="e^{(\cdot)}" />
    to get back to the original units, and compute your reported metric in those units. Standardizing the target is
    also common for neural networks so their last step starts at a sensible scale. For classification, the labels
    are categories and are not scaled; they are integer-coded, and the loss handles the rest.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Missing values.</strong> Models cannot multiply NaN. The standard approach is <span class="term">imputation</span>:
    fill numeric gaps with the training median (robust to outliers) or mean, and categorical gaps with the most
    frequent category or an explicit “missing” category. Fit the imputer on the training split, like a scaler. Add a
    binary <span class="term">missing indicator</span> column when missingness might carry meaning (a blank income
    field often does). Tree libraries such as XGBoost and LightGBM handle missing values natively by learning which
    way to send them at each split, which is one more reason they are the default for messy tabular data.
  </p>
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “For skewed positive targets I often model log y and invert at prediction time. Missing values are imputed with
      statistics fitted on the training split, typically the median, plus a missing indicator when absence is
      informative; gradient-boosted trees can handle missingness natively.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

